  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"A request board can list wanted item types without promising they will appear; visitors may leave a name or check the board themselves.\", \"decision\": \"How should requests for currently missing goods be recorded?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nA request volunteer asks visitors what they seek and checks the board against reserve stock and new contributions. One person connects requests with goods beyond the current display, making it easier to notice matches across different stations.\nVisitors write wanted item types on the request board, and table volunteers mark matching arrivals when they appear. Needs stay visible across the fair, and people can discover a later match without repeatedly asking every table.\nVisitors place wanted-item notes beside the relevant category signs, and table volunteers move matched notes to a collection point. Requests are seen where suitable goods are most likely to arrive, while the collection point gathers the resulting match notices."
    }
  ],
